          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01Us4hWEqBewsng5fi8VKjV9",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022: \u0022concepts/credential-and-path-composition.md\u0022, \u0022content\u0022: \u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Credential and Path Composition\\ndescription: How credential scoping and runtime-path centralization work together to establish a single trusted composition point.\\ntimestamp: 2025-01-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - source-type/synthesis\\n  - concept/Composition-Point\\n  - concept/Credential-Scoping\\n  - concept/Runtime-Paths\\nconfidence: high\\nconfidence_reason: \\\u0022Both source pages explicitly describe complementary mechanisms with the same underlying principle: centralize sensitive decisions at startup to ensure correctness and security downstream.\\\u0022\\nreview_date: 2025-07-14\\n---\\n\\n[[credential-scoping]] and [[runtime-paths]] implement the same architectural pattern at different levels: both centralize critical decisions at a single composition point (the Hub\u0027s startup sequence), then propagate the results to downstream processes and code in a controlled manner.\\n\\n**Credential Scoping** centralizes the decision about which secrets exist and how they are exposed: the Hub never holds API keys in its own environment. Instead, it reads the key once at startup and injects it only into the spawned child process\u0027s environment. This prevents a compromised Hub from leaking credentials through environment inspection \u2014 the key exists only in the ephemeral child process that needs it.\\n\\n**Runtime Paths** centralizes the decision about where every file lives: rather than allowing any part of the codebase to discover paths on its own (by reading the working directory or environment variables piecemeal), a single path resolver reads all location configuration at startup, validates that required inputs exist, auto-creates missing directories, and publishes the resolved path set to downstream code. No other part of the codebase rediscovers locations independently.\\n\\nBoth mechanisms achieve the same goal through the same strategy: make the critical decision once, in one place, at startup; publish only what is necessary downstream; and prevent distributed re-decision. This single-point-of-truth approach eliminates entire classes of errors (paths that are inconsistent, secrets that leak through careless environment handling) because the decision itself is not repeated \u2014 it is delegated to one authoritative source.\\n\\nThe two mechanisms are complementary parts of a larger principle: the Hub\u0027s startup sequence is the composition point for the entire Grimoire system. Secrets are composed there, paths are resolved there, and agent processes are spawned from that same point with their own scoped environments. This makes the Hub\u0027s startup sequence a natural place to reason about the entire system\u0027s initial state, rather than having configuration and credential logic scattered across multiple processes and startup phases.\\n\u0022}"
        }
