      "assistant_text": "It appears the index update is blocked by the safety policy. However, I have successfully created the synthesis page. Let me answer your question directly:\n\n## The Relationship Between Credential Scoping and Runtime-Path Decisions\n\nYour credential-scoping and runtime-path decisions are **complementary implementations of the same architectural principle**: both centralize critical decisions at the Hub\u0027s startup sequence and propagate the results downstream in a controlled manner.\n\n**Credential Scoping** ([[credential-scoping]]) works like this: the Hub never holds the Anthropic API key in its own process environment. Instead, at startup it reads the key from a git-ignored \u0060.env\u0060 file and injects it only into the spawned child process\u0027s environment. This prevents a compromised Hub from leaking the credential through environment inspection\u2014the key exists only in the ephemeral process that needs it.\n\n**Runtime Paths** ([[runtime-paths]]) works analogously: rather than allowing code to discover file locations scattered throughout the codebase (by reading the working directory directly), a single path resolver at startup reads all configuration, validates that required inputs exist, auto-creates missing directories, and publishes the complete resolved path set downstream. No other part of the codebase rediscovers paths independently.\n\nBoth mechanisms prevent distributed re-decision and eliminate entire classes of errors: paths that are inconsistent, secrets that leak through careless environment handling. Both achieve this by making the critical decision once, in one place, at startup\u2014the Hub\u0027s startup sequence itself becomes the composition point for the entire system.\n\n**I\u0027ve saved this connection as a new page**, [[concepts/credential-and-path-composition]], which explains how these two mechanisms work together to create a single authoritative composition point for Grimoire\u0027s initial state.",
